nodecode-long-conversations is a rewrite of cspears32's cspears32/hermes-long-conversations:

  https://github.com/cspears32/hermes-long-conversations
  commit 1a8dc52b7ae610f6db2d2ab0a7e7133b3a199807
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 cspears32 (declared in plugin.yaml; the repository carries no LICENSE file)

The rewritten plugin is the repository at that commit. Its own description
-- persistent long-term conversations across session boundaries via
structured handoff briefs -- is what this cell does, and the brief is the
original's: the same eight fields (topic, work in progress, decisions made,
active state files, key assumptions, open questions, dead ends, next step),
saved as one file per brief, listed newest first, read back as a block with
the same headings and the same closing line telling the next session to
continue from there. Its scoping rule is kept as well: a brief belongs to the
identity that wrote it, and a session reads only the briefs it may. The five
tools keep their names and their JSON answers -- handoff_save, handoff_load
and handoff_list answer the same success, message, brief, count and context
members the original's answered -- and the original's /long-conversations-free Hermes
install becomes a Nodecode slash command of that name.

What was rewritten, and how: the store is Common Lisp against the Nodecode
cell contract instead of Python against the Hermes plugin API. A brief is one
JSON file in the folder the section names, written atomically under the
folder's lock, instead of a profile directory of briefs, a config.yaml and a
lineage graph; the pre-LLM hook that used to inject a pending brief at a
session's first turn is one NLE:DEFINE-CELL :round observer, which hands a
saved brief to the first model request of another session and then clears the
marker. The optional beta event log is kept as the section's own reporting
rather than a second JSONL file, and the lineage graph is left to Nodecode,
whose sessions already record lineage.

What was left out, and why: the token-threshold monitor and the aggressive
auto-brief extraction -- the two hooks that watched the Hermes context
engine's last_total_tokens against its context_length, and at a watermark
either recommended a handoff or built one from the last three user messages.
Nodecode's own context editing and turn accounting do not expose that
watermark to a cell, so the brief is written when the model or the operator
decides instead of at a ratio; the eight brief fields, the tool answers and
the delivery are unaffected. The config tools (handoff_config_get and
handoff_config_set, which wrote a config.yaml beside the briefs) are the
section now: /settings and the `handoff` section of config.jsonc write the
same three values, so no tool is needed to change a switch the operator owns.
